[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Sharding Counter Patterns for High-Throughput Writes in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cloud Firestore is limited to roughly 1 write per second on a single document due to its underlying storage layer architecture. When building high-throughput applications like real-time analytics, voting systems, or gaming leaderboards, a single counter document will quickly become a bottleneck, resulting in contention errors and high latency."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem: Single Document Contention"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"If multiple clients simultaneously attempt to increment a standard counter document using FieldValue.increment(1), Firestore serializes these writes. As concurrency increases, transaction conflicts escalate, dramatically lowering your write throughput."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The Solution: Distributed Counter Sharding"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To bypass the 1 write per second limit, you can distribute the write load across multiple documents. This pattern, known as sharded counters, splits your single counter into multiple sub-documents. When you need the total count, you simply aggregate the values from all shards."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Sharded Counters"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, structure your Firestore collection to hold a parent document and a subcollection for the shards. Here is an example implementation in Node.js for incrementing a sharded counter."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const { initializeApp } = require('firebase-admin/app');
const { getFirestore, FieldValue } = require('firebase-admin/firestore');

initializeApp();
const db = getFirestore();

async function incrementCounter(counterId, numShards) {
  // Randomly select a shard between 0 and numShards - 1
  const shardId = Math.floor(Math.random() * numShards);
  const shardRef = db.collection('counters').doc(counterId).collection('shards').doc(shardId.toString());

  await shardRef.set({
    count: FieldValue.increment(1)
  }, { merge: true });
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Reading the Total Count"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because writes are distributed across shards, reading the total count requires querying all shard documents and summing their values. This operation can be performed periodically or cached to avoid read bottlenecks."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"async function getCount(counterId) {
  const shardsRef = db.collection('counters').doc(counterId).collection('shards');
  const snapshot = await shardsRef.get();

  let totalCount = 0;
  snapshot.forEach(doc => {
    const data = doc.data();
    if (data.count) {
      totalCount += data.count;
    }
  });

  return totalCount;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Sharded Counters"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Choose an appropriate shard count: Scale the number of shards based on your expected peak writes per second. A good rule of thumb is 10 to 50 shards for moderate-to-high throughput applications."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Cloud Functions for aggregation: If reads are frequent, use a background Cloud Function triggered by shard updates to maintain a cached total in the parent document."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Randomize shard selection: Ensure clients randomly choose a shard index on every write to distribute the load evenly and prevent hot-spotting on a single shard."}]
